Current original Zero workflow (0.12)

Use the native tools on Linux or their .exe versions on Windows:
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-build PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-check PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_TEST_DIRECTORY
All destination paths must be new. The integrator preserves authored sources;
the builder checks its compiler fixed point and builds the original targets;
the verifier checks language behavior and host file operations in process.
See tools/integrate.ztext, tools/build.ztext and checks/README.ztext.
Read these documents with zero-text read FILE.

The notes below preserve earlier implementation and test history. Any old
shell/Python runner names, generated artifacts, JSON reports or commands are
historical references: they were removed from the active project and are not
current runnable instructions or current-release validation. Native host
checks do not replace BIOS/UEFI, persistence or physical-hardware testing.

Historical implementation notes

Self-host compiler target regression corpus

Normal verification
From the project directory:
review-tests/selfhost-targets/run.sh build/zero

An optional second argument selects the result directory. Otherwise a fresh
directory under /tmp is used. The script compiles every committed fixture
with the candidate. It does not rebuild any compiler, invoke an assembler or
linker, or depend on a historical compiler binary. All test programs are Zero;
the shell scripts only orchestrate tools, files and comparisons.

expected.tsv is the committed oracle: 1107 target/fixture pairs, comprising
243 exact raw64/EFI64/raw32/EFI32/real output SHA-256 checks, 79 hosted-target
acceptance checks, and 785 rejection checks. A rejection requires status1 and
zero stdout. Diagnostics are retained but their wording is not compared.
The raw/EFI/real hashes include entry code and binary metadata, not merely
instruction bodies. Boundary cases include exact maximum output sizes.

Hosted ELF bytes are intentionally not compared with the historical output:
the self-host compiler expands the hosted allocation arena from64KiB to1MiB.
Five explicitly safe native tests execute string escapes/output, stdin/stdout,
and the full-u64 arithmetic/memory regression. Raw, EFI and real-mode binaries
are never executed by run.sh. The separate run-native.sh suite in the parent
review-tests directory covers additional hosted APIs and limits.

Fixture groups
positive/ contains common grammar, each builtin, nested calls, target-specific
names and complete u64/real-mode probes. invalid/ contains malformed arities,
unsupported operation forms and grammar failures. cases.tsv expresses the
historical target compatibility of these inputs.
boundary/ and boundaries.tsv cover source/string/name lengths, literal widths,
operator chains, comparison rejection, nesting, symbol counts and exact raw,
EFI, real and hosted output bounds. Fixtures are source files, not binaries.

Intentional unified-language change
The old separate target compilers did not reserve every other target's builtin
name. The unified compiler reserves all operation names consistently. Thus
23 former positive name cases deliberately become failures: @bios,
@protectedjump and @enable outside real mode, and @call7 outside IA32.
They remain in the suite; semantic-overrides.tsv identifies every such change.
These cases are not described as byte-parity successes. The current language
contract, not a hidden test omission, defines their expected rejection.

Baseline provenance and optional recapture
The non-host hashes and original acceptance/rejection baseline were captured
from three already-built original Zero compilers, copied to a private /tmp
directory before the self-host build replaced active binaries. No assembler
or linker ran during capture. REFERENCE-SHA256.ztext identifies those binaries;
the binaries themselves are not included here or needed by normal tests.

For an explicit historical audit only:
review-tests/selfhost-targets/record-baseline.sh REFERENCE_DIRECTORY

The supplied directory must contain zero-x64, zero-x86 and zero-real reference
executables. The script validates cases.tsv/boundaries.tsv against those
binaries, applies the documented semantic-overrides.tsv, and regenerates
expected.tsv. It does not build or obtain the references. Do not regenerate
expectations from an unreviewed candidate simply to make failures disappear.

Optional IA32 VM execution
review-tests/selfhost-targets/run-ia32-vm.sh build/zero build/zero.iso

This compiles the u64 probe with --raw32 and inserts it into a disposable COPY
of the documented17121280-byte optical image, at raw32 LBA104. The original
ISO and data disks are not modified; no data disk is attached. QEMU runs with
long mode and PAE disabled. Success requires exact ABCDEFGHIJKLMN0123OP plus
LF on debug portE9 and the expected test-only exit-device status.
The probe covers carry/borrow, wrapping arithmetic, cross-half multiplication,
negation, unsigned high/low comparisons, high-only truth, all load/store widths,
strings, loop code and the IA32 entry argument. This is external VM test
instrumentation, not a production OS syscall.

ZERO_QEMU, ZERO_QEMU_DATA and ZERO_BIOS_ROM may select the installed test tools.
The current workspace's privately extracted QEMU tree is detected as a fallback.
ZERO_TEST_TIMEOUT and ZERO_VM_TIMEOUT adjust bounded test execution time.
All result binaries, diagnostics and transcripts remain in the selected result
directory for review. Passing VM tests does not establish physical-hardware
or native VirtualBox compatibility.
